1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,942
A Boeing 737 is sliding down a Korean runway on its belly, backwards along the strip, with no wheels down.

2
00:00:08,066 --> 00:00:11,580
It is the 29th of December, 2024.

3
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:13,630
Three minutes past nine in the morning.

4
00:00:14,766 --> 00:00:16,107
It is running out of runway.

5
00:00:16,107 --> 00:00:20,800
250 metres past the end of it there is a low grassy mound with an aerial on top.

6
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:24,049
Inside that mound is concrete.

7
00:00:25,166 --> 00:00:29,533
179 of the 181 people on board will die.

8
00:00:29,533 --> 00:00:34,413
And a simulation commissioned by their own government will later conclude that every one of them could have survived.

9
00:00:35,533 --> 00:00:37,360
This is not a film about pilot error.

10
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:44,441
It is about a mound of earth with concrete in it, a rule that said it should have come apart, and 22 years of paperwork that said it was fine.

11
00:00:45,566 --> 00:00:49,432
And it is about four minutes and seven seconds that nobody has a recording of.

12
00:00:50,566 --> 00:00:53,074
Jeju Air 2216.

13
00:00:54,200 --> 00:00:55,767
Bangkok to Muan.

14
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:02,113
The board's own file number for this is AAR2404.

15
00:01:02,113 --> 00:01:09,413
A Boeing 737-800, registration HL8088, serial number 37541.

16
00:01:10,533 --> 00:01:14,808
One correction before we go on, because almost every comment thread gets it wrong.

17
00:01:14,808 --> 00:01:17,251
This is not a 737 Max.

18
00:01:17,251 --> 00:01:23,359
It is the previous generation, the 800, and the difference matters the moment somebody reaches for the Max story.

19
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:29,888
Something in the report I did not expect. This aircraft was not delivered new to Jeju Air.

20
00:01:29,888 --> 00:01:33,058
It went to Ryanair, in September 2009.

21
00:01:33,058 --> 00:01:36,228
Jeju Air leased it in February 2017.

22
00:01:37,366 --> 00:01:42,042
It is 15 years old. For an airliner that is middle aged, not old.

23
00:01:43,166 --> 00:01:48,417
175 passengers. Six crew. 181 people.

24
00:01:49,566 --> 00:01:57,194
It leaves Bangkok in the dark and it is due into Muan International, on the south western coast of Korea, a little after nine in the morning.

25
00:01:58,333 --> 00:01:59,485
The crew are experienced.

26
00:01:59,485 --> 00:02:08,123
The captain has 6,823 hours, 6,000 of them on this type, and two and a half thousand of those in command of it.

27
00:02:08,123 --> 00:02:11,002
The first officer has 1,650.

28
00:02:12,133 --> 00:02:18,588
And the weather is, frankly, lovely. Wind from 110 degrees at two knots.

29
00:02:18,588 --> 00:02:25,043
Nine kilometres of visibility. A few clouds at four and a half thousand feet. Two degrees.

30
00:02:25,043 --> 00:02:26,657
No significant change expected.

31
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:30,491
Nothing about the sky that morning is working against them.

32
00:02:31,633 --> 00:02:36,995
At 54 minutes and 43 seconds past eight, the aircraft calls Muan tower for landing.

33
00:02:36,995 --> 00:02:40,149
The tower clears it to land on runway 01.

34
00:02:41,266 --> 00:02:46,673
At 57 minutes and 50 seconds past eight, the tower tells them to be cautious of bird activity.

35
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,353
Five minutes and seven seconds later the aircraft is in the embankment.

36
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:53,806
Baikal teal.

37
00:02:54,933 --> 00:02:57,850
The bird is a Baikal teal. A migratory duck.

38
00:02:57,850 --> 00:03:03,684
Enormous flocks of them winter in that part of Korea, in numbers that are genuinely hard to picture.

39
00:03:04,833 --> 00:03:07,211
The report is careful about how it knows.

40
00:03:07,211 --> 00:03:09,887
The pilots identified a group of birds on approach.

41
00:03:09,887 --> 00:03:14,942
A security camera filmed the aircraft coming close to a group of birds during the go around.

42
00:03:16,066 --> 00:03:19,668
Both engines were examined. Feathers and blood were found on each.

43
00:03:19,668 --> 00:03:25,235
Samples went out for DNA analysis, and a domestic organisation identified them as Baikal teals.

44
00:03:26,366 --> 00:03:27,777
Both. Not one.

45
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:33,149
A twin engine airliner is built to fly on one. That is the whole design philosophy.

46
00:03:33,149 --> 00:03:38,461
What it is not built for is losing thrust on both at 500 feet with no height to trade.

47
00:03:39,599 --> 00:03:41,716
Four minutes, seven seconds.

48
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:47,979
This channel is called Black Box Breakdown, and this is the part of this accident I cannot get past.

49
00:03:49,099 --> 00:03:51,898
Both recorders were fitted and both were working.

50
00:03:51,898 --> 00:03:57,145
And then, at 58 minutes and 50 seconds past eight, both of them stopped.

51
00:03:58,266 --> 00:04:02,902
The aircraft hit the embankment at two minutes and 57 seconds past nine.

52
00:04:02,902 --> 00:04:04,889
The report states the arithmetic plainly.

53
00:04:04,889 --> 00:04:08,532
The last four minutes and seven seconds of recording are missing.

54
00:04:09,666 --> 00:04:13,010
It even tells you where the aeroplane was when the recording ended.

55
00:04:13,010 --> 00:04:16,353
161 knots. 498 feet.

56
00:04:17,499 --> 00:04:20,394
Not burned. Not crushed. Not unreadable.

57
00:04:20,394 --> 00:04:24,735
They stopped, together, while the aircraft was still flying.

58
00:04:25,866 --> 00:04:29,372
And here is the detail that reorganised this whole film for me.

59
00:04:29,372 --> 00:04:33,755
The mayday call is logged at 58 minutes and 56 seconds past eight.

60
00:04:34,899 --> 00:04:37,093
Six seconds after the recorders stopped.

61
00:04:38,233 --> 00:04:41,724
I had this the wrong way round before I read the primary document.

62
00:04:41,724 --> 00:04:45,215
I assumed the emergency came first and the recorders died in whatever followed.

63
00:04:45,215 --> 00:04:46,827
It is the other way round.

64
00:04:47,966 --> 00:04:52,333
The voice recorder was recovered intact and a transcript was made on the fourth of January.

65
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:58,337
The data recorder had a severed connector and was flown to the National Transportation Safety Board in Washington to be read out.

66
00:04:59,466 --> 00:05:02,627
Both of them are missing the same four minutes and seven seconds.

67
00:05:03,766 --> 00:05:10,466
The leading theory, and I want to be careful because it is a theory, is a total loss of electrical power.

68
00:05:10,466 --> 00:05:16,208
That explanation was offered by Sim Jai dong, a former transport ministry investigator, in coverage at the time.

69
00:05:16,208 --> 00:05:18,760
It has not been confirmed as the cause.

70
00:05:19,899 --> 00:05:24,144
Now hold what is inside that window. The go around. The turn back.

71
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:28,063
The decision to land the other way. Whatever happened with the gear.

72
00:05:28,063 --> 00:05:29,696
Whatever happened with the engines.

73
00:05:30,833 --> 00:05:33,341
All of it is in the four minutes nobody has.

74
00:05:34,466 --> 00:05:39,220
Everything anyone has concluded about those minutes was inferred from something other than the black boxes.

75
00:05:40,366 --> 00:05:44,350
One more thing about that document, and I think it is to the board's credit.

76
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:48,333
On every single page of the preliminary report there is a line in small type.

77
00:05:49,466 --> 00:05:53,136
This is preliminary information, subject to change, and may contain errors.

78
00:05:53,136 --> 00:05:58,139
Any errors in this report will be corrected when the final report has been completed.

79
00:05:59,266 --> 00:06:00,806
They wrote that on every page.

80
00:06:00,806 --> 00:06:03,628
I would ask you to hold it against everything that followed.

81
00:06:04,766 --> 00:06:11,114
It is worth being concrete about what is in those four minutes, because black box is a phrase people use without picturing it.

82
00:06:12,233 --> 00:06:15,439
A flight data recorder is not a summary.

83
00:06:15,439 --> 00:06:21,050
It is a continuous stream of hundreds of parameters, sampled several times a second.

84
00:06:21,050 --> 00:06:25,057
Control positions. Engine parameters for each engine separately. Gear position.

85
00:06:25,057 --> 00:06:27,462
Flap position. Every warning that sounded.

86
00:06:28,599 --> 00:06:31,245
A cockpit voice recorder is four channels.

87
00:06:31,245 --> 00:06:35,025
Each pilot's microphone, the cockpit area microphone, and the radio.

88
00:06:36,166 --> 00:06:41,730
Between them, those two boxes would have answered almost every question anybody has asked about this accident.

89
00:06:42,866 --> 00:06:45,746
Which engine was producing thrust, and which one was not.

90
00:06:45,746 --> 00:06:49,490
What the crew said to each other about it. Whether a warning sounded.

91
00:06:49,490 --> 00:06:54,673
Whether the gear was selected and did not come. What they were trying to do in the turn.

92
00:06:55,799 --> 00:07:01,285
All of it was being written down, continuously, until 58 minutes and 50 seconds past eight.

93
00:07:02,433 --> 00:07:04,235
And then it was not.

94
00:07:05,366 --> 00:07:07,404
19, the wrong way.

95
00:07:08,533 --> 00:07:11,929
They had been approaching runway 01. They go around.

96
00:07:13,066 --> 00:07:17,622
And then they do something that tells you exactly how much time they believed they had.

97
00:07:17,622 --> 00:07:24,455
As the report puts it, the aircraft was flying over the left side of runway 01, turned right, and approached runway 19.

98
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:30,354
Same strip of tarmac. Opposite direction. No circuit, no second thoughts.

99
00:07:31,500 --> 00:07:34,581
The landing gear is not down. It never comes down.

100
00:07:35,699 --> 00:07:40,846
The aircraft touches down on its belly, roughly 1,200 metres along the runway.

101
00:07:41,966 --> 00:07:47,034
And I want to be very clear about this next part, because it is the hinge of the entire story.

102
00:07:48,166 --> 00:07:50,624
A belly landing on a runway is survivable.

103
00:07:50,624 --> 00:07:53,388
Aircraft have done it and people have walked away.

104
00:07:53,388 --> 00:07:57,074
The airframe slides, the friction takes the energy out, and it stops.

105
00:07:58,199 --> 00:08:00,707
This one was still moving when it ran out of tarmac.

106
00:08:01,833 --> 00:08:03,714
250 metres.

107
00:08:04,833 --> 00:08:10,291
Past the end of runway 19 there is a structure carrying the aerial array for the instrument landing system.

108
00:08:10,291 --> 00:08:10,837
The localiser.

109
00:08:10,837 --> 00:08:15,204
The part that tells an approaching aircraft whether it is left or right of the centreline.

110
00:08:16,333 --> 00:08:22,864
It sits about 250 metres beyond the runway end, and with the aerials on top it stands around four metres tall.

111
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:28,283
From the air it is a low grassy mound. That is genuinely what it looks like.

112
00:08:29,433 --> 00:08:31,627
Inside it there is a concrete structure.

113
00:08:31,627 --> 00:08:35,389
And in 2023, a concrete slab was added.

114
00:08:36,533 --> 00:08:40,545
The report's description of the wreckage is the part I would ask you to sit with.

115
00:08:40,545 --> 00:08:42,802
Both engines were buried in the embankment's soil mound.

116
00:08:43,933 --> 00:08:48,436
The forward fuselage scattered between 30 and 200 metres from the embankment.

117
00:08:48,436 --> 00:08:51,900
The tail flipped and came down beyond it, partly burning.

118
00:08:53,033 --> 00:08:56,907
The two survivors were cabin crew, in the rear jump seats, in the tail.

119
00:08:56,907 --> 00:09:00,504
The part of the aeroplane that ended up past the thing it hit.

120
00:09:01,633 --> 00:09:05,029
They were both seriously injured. Everyone forward of them died.

121
00:09:06,166 --> 00:09:11,155
It is worth knowing what a localiser actually does, because it explains why it was sitting there at all.

122
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:16,223
It transmits a pair of overlapping radio beams straight down the runway centreline.

123
00:09:16,223 --> 00:09:23,768
An aircraft on approach compares them, and the needle in the cockpit tells the crew they are left, right, or exactly where they should be.

124
00:09:24,900 --> 00:09:29,994
For that to work it has to sit in line with the runway, at the far end. Not near it.

125
00:09:29,994 --> 00:09:31,013
In line with it.

126
00:09:32,133 --> 00:09:36,146
The design document has a phrase for equipment like this. Fixed by function.

127
00:09:36,146 --> 00:09:42,321
A navigation aid that must be positioned in a particular location in order to provide an essential benefit for aviation.

128
00:09:43,466 --> 00:09:48,004
So nobody is arguing it should have been somewhere else. It could not be somewhere else.

129
00:09:48,004 --> 00:09:49,422
That is the whole point.

130
00:09:50,566 --> 00:09:52,447
The standard does not say move it.

131
00:09:52,447 --> 00:09:56,208
The standard says that because it has to be there, it has to break.

132
00:09:57,333 --> 00:10:02,518
I have not been able to establish much about the two who lived, and I am not going to invent it.

133
00:10:02,518 --> 00:10:07,704
What the report records is that they were crew, that they were seriously injured, and that they were in the tail.

134
00:10:08,833 --> 00:10:18,237
Whether they were seated there by roster, by rank, or by whatever ordinary arrangement puts a particular person in a particular seat on a particular morning, I do not know.

135
00:10:19,366 --> 00:10:21,168
It decided whether they lived.

136
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:23,293
Frangible.

137
00:10:24,433 --> 00:10:30,546
There is a word in airport design that decides this entire accident, and most people watching will never have needed to know it.

138
00:10:31,666 --> 00:10:32,001
Frangible.

139
00:10:32,001 --> 00:10:38,353
Here is how the American regulator defines it, and the international standard says the same thing in different words.

140
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:51,307
Retains its structural integrity and stiffness up to a designated maximum load, but on impact from a greater load, breaks, distorts, or yields in such a manner as to present the minimum hazard to aircraft.

141
00:10:52,433 --> 00:10:55,391
Read it again, because it is doing something very specific.

142
00:10:55,391 --> 00:10:57,757
It is not asking for a strong structure.

143
00:10:57,757 --> 00:11:01,602
It is requiring that past a certain load, the thing must give way.

144
00:11:02,733 --> 00:11:04,705
The rule exists for exactly one reason.

145
00:11:04,705 --> 00:11:10,622
Aeroplanes sometimes leave the end of runways, and the things sitting near a runway have to break rather than stop them.

146
00:11:11,766 --> 00:11:18,114
An aerial on a breakaway mast does its job perfectly well in normal use, and folds when 70 tonnes arrives.

147
00:11:19,233 --> 00:11:20,957
Concrete does not fold.

148
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:30,850
In December 2025, South Korea's Anti Corruption and Civil Rights Commission ruled that the embankment at Muan violated frangibility safety requirements.

149
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:36,754
Not that it was unlucky. Not that it was borderline. That it violated the requirement.

150
00:11:37,899 --> 00:11:46,024
And there is a second thing in that same document, which I did not know before I read it, and which I think is the quietest and worst fact in this entire story.

151
00:11:47,166 --> 00:11:50,115
The ground past the end of a runway is not spare ground.

152
00:11:50,115 --> 00:11:52,573
It has a name, and it has a defined purpose.

153
00:11:53,699 --> 00:11:55,893
It is called the runway safety area.

154
00:11:57,033 --> 00:12:06,829
A defined surface surrounding the runway, prepared or suitable for reducing the risk of damage to aircraft in the event of an undershoot, overshoot, or excursion from the runway.

155
00:12:07,966 --> 00:12:09,804
Read what that ground is for.

156
00:12:09,804 --> 00:12:13,480
It exists specifically for the case where an aeroplane leaves the runway.

157
00:12:13,480 --> 00:12:18,075
That is not an unforeseen event. It is the event the surface is designed around.

158
00:12:19,199 --> 00:12:22,830
It is the one piece of an airport whose entire job is to be survivable.

159
00:12:23,966 --> 00:12:27,937
And 250 metres into it, at Muan, there was concrete.

160
00:12:29,066 --> 00:12:33,350
A caveat I owe you, because I have been quoting an American document at a Korean airport.

161
00:12:34,499 --> 00:12:37,007
The Federal Aviation Administration does not regulate Muan.

162
00:12:37,007 --> 00:12:43,590
I am using its wording because it is public, it is precise, and it is free to show you on screen.

163
00:12:44,733 --> 00:12:53,398
The standard that actually binds a Korean airport is the international one, through the civil aviation organisation, and it requires the same thing in its own words.

164
00:12:53,398 --> 00:12:55,966
Equipment sited near a runway must be frangible.

165
00:12:57,099 --> 00:12:59,988
I am not relying on my paraphrase of that.

166
00:12:59,988 --> 00:13:08,332
I am relying on what Korea's own anti corruption commission concluded in December 2025, which is that the embankment violated frangibility requirements.

167
00:13:09,466 --> 00:13:10,772
22 years.

168
00:13:11,899 --> 00:13:16,418
If this were only Muan, it would be a story about one airport and one bad decision.

169
00:13:17,566 --> 00:13:26,474
In March 2026, a state audit reported that 14 non compliant localiser installations, at eight airports, had been wrongly approved.

170
00:13:27,599 --> 00:13:31,726
The list included Muan. It also included Jeju, and Gimhae.

171
00:13:32,866 --> 00:13:39,135
And they had been carrying certified safety inspections for up to 22 years, while failing frangibility requirements.

172
00:13:40,266 --> 00:13:43,740
22 years of somebody signing a piece of paper that said this is fine.

173
00:13:44,866 --> 00:13:47,054
As for why concrete was there at all.

174
00:13:47,054 --> 00:13:53,617
The reporting on the state findings says the ground beneath the runway safety area slopes away, and levelling it would have meant major earthworks.

175
00:13:54,766 --> 00:13:59,207
So instead of moving the earth, they raised the aerial on a structure. It was cheaper.

176
00:14:00,333 --> 00:14:01,169
All of them.

177
00:14:02,299 --> 00:14:12,408
On the eighth of January, 2026, the Korean broadcaster SBS and the New York Times revealed a report the families had been asking to see and had not been given.

178
00:14:13,533 --> 00:14:16,459
A computer simulation, commissioned by the government.

179
00:14:17,599 --> 00:14:25,723
Its conclusion was that if the localiser and its supports had not been there, or had been built from frangible materials, everyone on board would have survived.

180
00:14:26,866 --> 00:14:32,860
I want to be precise, because this is the strongest claim in this film and it does not need any help from me.

181
00:14:32,860 --> 00:14:34,944
The finding is that they could have survived.

182
00:14:34,944 --> 00:14:37,550
It is a simulation, and it carries a simulation's assumptions.

183
00:14:38,699 --> 00:14:45,465
It is still a government commissioned finding that the structure is the reason the number is 179, rather than something close to zero.

184
00:14:46,599 --> 00:14:52,320
And the families had asked for it, and had not been shown it, until a journalist put it in a newspaper.

185
00:14:53,466 --> 00:14:54,459
Withdrawn.

186
00:14:55,599 --> 00:15:00,432
Now the part most coverage of this accident handles badly, and I would rather go slowly here.

187
00:15:01,566 --> 00:15:08,332
On the nineteenth of July, 2025, investigators presented interim findings to the victims' families.

188
00:15:09,466 --> 00:15:17,094
Those findings said the crew had shut down the left engine, which was relatively undamaged, rather than the right, which was the one the birds had destroyed.

189
00:15:18,233 --> 00:15:19,220
That finding is disputed.

190
00:15:19,220 --> 00:15:28,107
I am not going to tell you it is true and I am not going to tell you it is false, because the investigation has not finished and the people who would know have not published.

191
00:15:29,233 --> 00:15:35,581
What I can tell you is that it is an interim finding, and that the black boxes stop four minutes and seven seconds before impact.

192
00:15:36,699 --> 00:15:39,781
The families rejected it. So did the pilots' union.

193
00:15:40,899 --> 00:15:43,647
Their objection was not simply that it blamed the crew.

194
00:15:43,647 --> 00:15:49,415
It was that it made the crew's actions the story, while the barrier the aircraft actually hit was treated as background.

195
00:15:50,533 --> 00:15:53,316
And then something happened that almost never happens.

196
00:15:53,316 --> 00:15:57,142
The scheduled release of those findings was withdrawn, after the protests.

197
00:15:58,266 --> 00:16:03,673
The board agreed to widen the inquiry beyond pilot error, into infrastructure and systemic factors.

198
00:16:04,799 --> 00:16:11,228
Which, to be fair to the board, is what its own preliminary report had already said it would do.

199
00:16:11,228 --> 00:16:15,287
Tear down the engines. Analyse the recorders and the air traffic data.

200
00:16:15,287 --> 00:16:18,670
Investigate the embankment, the localisers, and the bird strike evidence.

201
00:16:19,799 --> 00:16:26,721
It also says the investigation is being assisted by the National Transportation Safety Board in the United States and the BEA in France.

202
00:16:27,866 --> 00:16:32,606
On the second of December, 2025, the interim report was delayed.

203
00:16:32,606 --> 00:16:37,662
International rules require an interim update when a final report is not finished within a year.

204
00:16:38,799 --> 00:16:44,755
On the 22nd of December, South Korea's parliament passed a bill authorising an independent investigation.

205
00:16:45,899 --> 00:16:49,820
The final report was expected around the middle of 2026.

206
00:16:49,820 --> 00:16:52,900
As this film is made, it has still not been published.

207
00:16:54,033 --> 00:16:54,791
The record.

208
00:16:55,933 --> 00:16:58,937
Strip out the argument and here is what is not in dispute.

209
00:17:00,066 --> 00:17:06,675
On a clear morning with two knots of wind, a duck went into both engines of a serviceable aircraft, five minutes from the ground.

210
00:17:07,799 --> 00:17:17,830
Both flight recorders stopped together, four minutes and seven seconds before the end, at 161 knots and 498 feet, and nobody has yet said why with certainty.

211
00:17:18,966 --> 00:17:24,452
An experienced crew put the aircraft down on a runway, on its belly, which is a thing people survive.

212
00:17:25,599 --> 00:17:29,726
And it slid into concrete that an international standard said should have broken apart.

213
00:17:30,866 --> 00:17:34,328
A state commission has since ruled that it violated the requirement.

214
00:17:34,328 --> 00:17:37,475
A government simulation says it is the reason everyone died.

215
00:17:38,599 --> 00:17:41,107
179 people.

216
00:17:42,233 --> 00:17:46,674
14 installations. Eight airports. 22 years of approvals.

217
00:17:47,799 --> 00:17:49,200
None of it is hidden.

218
00:17:49,200 --> 00:17:55,923
It is in a preliminary report, a commission ruling, a state audit, and a simulation that had to be prised out through a newspaper.

219
00:17:57,066 --> 00:17:59,372
What I keep coming back to is not the engines.

220
00:17:59,372 --> 00:18:03,753
It is not even the four minutes, though I would very much like to know what is on them.

221
00:18:04,899 --> 00:18:07,039
It is that the rule was already written.

222
00:18:07,039 --> 00:18:13,990
Somebody worked out, decades ago, that aircraft sometimes run off the end, and that whatever sits near a runway has to give way when they do.

223
00:18:15,133 --> 00:18:18,294
The rule was there. The paperwork said it had been followed.

224
00:18:19,433 --> 00:18:20,739
And it had not been.

225
00:18:21,866 --> 00:18:29,102
If you want the rest of this, the final report is still to come, and when it lands I will go through it here line by line, the same way.

226
00:18:30,233 --> 00:18:33,629
Subscribe if you want that one, because it is the only way it reaches you.

227
00:18:34,766 --> 00:18:45,920
And if you want the other side of this coin, there is a film on this channel about an order that was given in a bunker in Washington, correctly, by a man who was not in the chain of command, and which reached no pilot at all.

228
00:18:47,066 --> 00:18:48,992
Same failure, running the opposite way.

229
00:18:48,992 --> 00:18:54,768
That video is called Cheney Said Engage, Nobody Told the Pilots, and it is on the channel now.

230
00:18:54,768 --> 00:18:56,052
Watch that one next.

